    "raw_output_sha256": "b462815b026d368c87de85bef246111530f850866b93c37693f07fdffec80004",
    "score_q": 96,
    "span_selector": {
     "method": "bm25/1",
     "params": {
      "b_x1000": 750,
      "chunk_chars": 1000,
      "k1_x1000": 1200,
      "overlap_chars": 200
     },
     "version": "1"
    },
    "spans": [
     {
      "end": 518,
      "start": 0,
      "text": "Mouse Spermatogenic Stem Cells Continually Interconvert between Equipotent Singly Isolated and Syncytial States\nThe identity and behavior of mouse spermatogenic stem cells have been a long-standing focus of interest. In the prevailing \"As model,\" stem cell function is restricted to singly isolated (As) spermatogonia. By examining single-cell dynamics of GFR\u03b11+ stem cells in vivo, we evaluate an alternative hypothesis that, through fragmentation, syncytial spermatogonia also contribute to stem cell function in hom"
     },
     {
      "end": 1037,
      "start": 518,
      "text": "eostasis. We use live imaging and pulse labeling to quantitatively determine the fates of individual GFR\u03b11+ cells and find that, during steady-state spermatogenesis, the entire GFR\u03b11+ population comprises a single stem cell pool, in which cells continually interconvert between As and syncytial states. A minimal biophysical model, relying only on the rates of incomplete cell division and syncytial fragmentation, precisely predicts the stochastic fates of GFR\u03b11+ cells during steady state and postinsult regeneration."
     }
    ],
    "status": "ok"
   },
